    <!-- FAQ -->
    <section class="cg-section cg-faq-section" id="faq">
      <div class="cg-faq-intro">
        <span class="cg-section-label">NEED HELP?</span>
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <p>
          Learn more about source-code submissions and similarity analysis.
        </p>
        
      </div>

      <div class="cg-faq-list">
        <details open>
          <summary>How does CodeGuard check code similarity?</summary>
          <p>
            CodeGuard's C++ pipeline can preprocess source code, tokenize
            it, generate fingerprints, and store them for comparison.
            Actual similarity results depend on the comparison logic
            connected to the application.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>Can two different programs have similar code?</summary>
          <p>
            Yes. Students may independently use the same standard
            algorithm, syntax, or programming pattern. Similarity results
            should be reviewed in context.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>Which file formats can I submit?</summary>
          <p>
            The current submission form accepts .cpp, .c, and .java files.
            The maximum upload size is 1 MB per file.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>Are my submissions stored?</summary>
          <p>
            Your current application saves submission details in MongoDB
            and stores uploaded files in the project's data directory.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>Does a similarity match prove plagiarism?</summary>
          <p>
            No. A match is a signal for further review. An educator
            should examine the code, assignment requirements, and context
            before drawing conclusions.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>How do I submit my code?</summary>
          <p>
            Open the Submit Code page, enter your student details, choose
            an assignment and language, select a source file, and submit
            the form.
          </p>
          <a href="/listings/new">Go to Submit Code →</a>
        </details>
      </div>
    </section>
